--TEST--
An integer literal too large for an `int` is a `uint` wherever it is written, including where the expected type never reaches the literal — `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The checker's magnitude rule: digits past `int`'s range are legal exactly
// where `uint` is expected, so by the time one of them is compiled there is
// only one type it can have. Both sides of that boundary, on a binding that
// states the type itself.
int $last = 9223372036854775807;
uint $first = 9223372036854775808;
uint $largest = 18446744073709551615;
echo $last, " ", $first, " ", $largest, "\n";

// The same three inside an array literal, which is the shape that has to read
// the magnitude rather than the expectation: an element is compiled as an
// ordinary expression, so nothing hands it the `uint` the annotation states.
array<uint> $asks = [0, 9223372036854775807, 9223372036854775808, 18446744073709551615];
foreach ($asks as uint $ask) {
    echo $ask, "\n";
}

// And as an argument, where the expectation does reach it, so the two paths
// agree on the answer rather than only on the boundary.
class Ask {
    public static function ceiling(uint $bytes): uint {
        return $bytes;
    }
}
echo Ask::ceiling(18446744073709551615), "\n";
echo $asks[3] == Ask::ceiling(18446744073709551615) ? "the two paths agree\n" : "they disagree\n";
--EXPECT--
9223372036854775807 9223372036854775808 18446744073709551615
0
9223372036854775807
9223372036854775808
18446744073709551615
18446744073709551615
the two paths agree
